#{[17592186362294 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362294 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98347" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362294 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362294 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & f(3,0,4)=25, &  & {f}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{5x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}, \\\\  & {f}_{y}(x,y,z)=\\frac{5y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}, &  & {f}_{z}(x,y,z)=\\frac{5z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,0,4),$ estimate the value of $f(3.01,0.1,3.99).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362294 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "093c7065-0aaa-5dd8-919e-df8aa64caef0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362294 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,0,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,0,4)+∇f(3,0,4)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-0,z-4⟩ \\\\  & =25+⟨{f}_{x}(3,0,4),{f}_{y}(3,0,4),{f}_{z}(3,0,4)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y,z-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(3,0,4)$, ${f}_{y}(3,0,4)$ and ${f}_{z}(3,0,4),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,0,4) & =\\frac{5(3)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=3 \\\\ {f}_{y}(3,0,4) & =\\frac{5(0)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=0 \\\\ {f}_{z}(3,0,4) & =\\frac{5(4)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈25+⟨3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y,z-4⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(3.01,0.1,3.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=3.01,y=0.1$ and $z=3.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.01,0.1,3.99) & ≈25+⟨3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨3.01-3,0.1,3.99-4⟩ \\\\  & =25+⟨3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.1,-0.01⟩ \\\\  & =25+0.03-0.04 \\\\  & =24.99\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385899 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385899 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98328" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385899 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385899 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(-1,-2)=4,\\,{f}_{x}(-1,-2)=4,\\,{f}_{y}(-1,-2)=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,-2),$ estimate the value of $f(-1.05,-1.96).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385899 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cde219f4-dfdd-5794-bbe7-bc4ef32852ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385899 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,-2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,-2)+∇f(-1,-2)\\cdot ⟨x+1,y+2⟩ \\\\  & =f(-1,-2)+⟨{f}_{x}(-1,-2),{f}_{y}(-1,-2)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y+2⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨4,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y+2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1.05,-1.96)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-1.05$ and $y=-1.96,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1.05,-1.96) & ≈4+⟨4,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-1.05+1,-1.96+2⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨4,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.05,0.04⟩ \\\\  & =4-0.20-0.08 \\\\  & =3.72\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385900 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385900 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385900 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186385904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385900 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "21a9cbd3-1fa3-54d9-8a88-b7bd246ff005" #edb/ref 17