926 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420926 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "df1e2f42-abc0-5025-a335-6ed1280fc719" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420927 #edb/ref 1080 "f(x,y,z)≈-1-x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420927 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420927 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f7a0590c-c153-5bd3-ab0a-b20fc19d4881" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420928 #edb/ref 1080 "f(x,y)≈-1-2x-4y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420928 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420928 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe73bc79-52e6-53a8-9039-023077c29e7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420929 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420929 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420929 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420929 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "71f2f20d-59aa-5f4b-b243-cfacb310155a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420930 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420930 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129741" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420930 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420930 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420930 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55146915-2d47-5b59-9386-37303589ba19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420930 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,0)+∇f(-1,0)\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(-1,0)=1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =2x{e}^{y}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(-1,0)=-2, \\\\ {f}_{y} & ={x}^{2}{e}^{y} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(-1,0)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(-1,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,0) & =⟨-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,0)+∇f(-1,0)\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩ \\\\  & ≈1-2(x+1)+y \\\\  & ≈-1-2x+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420931 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186420935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420931 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "271dc584-8067-5c79-8663-42f0cf534fe1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420931 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186362293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420932 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "428b58fe-7d44-5904-bbbf-8158fe8c3758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420932 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186420930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420933 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186420934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420933 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac2d8892-990a-561c-b817-20d63c4e20b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420933 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420934 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186420931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420934 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "de8e0d3a-aa28-5870-806b-fac6775a1e3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420934 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420935 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186420932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420935 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9443769-9265-54cf-81f2-24a4db8a375c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420935 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186265764 #edb/ref 1759218604