ref 17592186045417] [17592186425852 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(-2,0)=-3,\\,{f}_{x}(-2,0)=1,\\,{f}_{y}(-2,0)=0.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-2,0),$ estimate the value of $f(-1.99,0.01).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425852 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3702a170-bd38-5307-a582-a3c4941c804b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425852 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-2,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-2,0)+∇f(-2,0)\\cdot ⟨x-(-2),y-0⟩ \\\\  & =f(-2,0)+⟨{f}_{x}(-2,0),{f}_{y}(-2,0)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+2,y⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+2,y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1.99,0.01)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-1.99$ and $y=0.01,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1.99,0.01) & ≈-3+⟨1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1.99+2,0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-3+1(0.01)+0(0.01) \\\\  & =-3+0.01+0 \\\\  & =-2.99\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425854 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98327" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425854 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425854 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(3,-1)=-3,\\,{f}_{x}(3,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{y}(3,-1)=1.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(3.01,-0.97).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbf719bd-56e4-5b5f-a665-42577dad1834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425854 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(3,-1)+∇f(3,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =f(3,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(3,-1),{f}_{y}(3,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(3.01,-0.97)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=3.01$ and $y=-0.97,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.01,-0.97) & ≈-3+⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨3.01-3,-0.97+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.03⟩ \\\\  & =-3-0.01+0.03 \\\\  & =-2.98\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428468 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428468 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: differential=1 (1/1); foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1937->1934. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428468 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050143 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428469 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428469 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: differential=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity